# .trivyignore — accepted-risk suppressions for Trivy scans.
#
# ONE LINE PER FINDING. Every line must carry a comment stating who accepted
# the risk and why. A bare CVE ID with no justification is not reviewable and
# should fail review.
#
# FORMAT
#   <ID>[:<field>:<value>] [# <justification> @<approver> exp:YYYY-MM-DD]
#
#   <ID>          CVE-XXXX-YYYY (vuln) or AVD-XXX-0000 (misconfig) or
#                 secret/<rule-id>
#   exp:YYYY-MM-DD  drops the suppression entirely on this date. There is no
#                 grace period; a stale line stops suppressing and the finding
#                 returns. This is the whole point — an unbounded ignore is how
#                 three-year-old CVEs end up in production.
#
# HOW TO CHOOSE A SUPPRESSION
#   1. Not exploitable in this context?      -> add a line here with a comment.
#   2. Fix exists but not yet scheduled?     -> add a line with a near expiry;
#                                               the CVE reappears when the date
#                                               passes, which forces the
#                                               conversation.
#   3. Genuinely not affected (dead code, unreachable path, patched upstream)?
#                                            -> publish a VEX document instead
#                                               (--vex). A VEX statement is
#                                               attached to the artifact and
#                                               reviewable by anyone consuming
#                                               it; an ignore line is invisible
#                                               outside this repo.
#
# REVIEW CADENCE: quarterly. Remove entries whose exp: has passed, whose fix
# has shipped, or whose compensating control is gone.
#
# No suppressions are currently recorded.

# CVE-2024-0000 # example — <why> @<approver> exp:2026-12-31